{
  "dom_id": "step-q51072",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x+4, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ x+2, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(-2,2)$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$",
  "worked_solution": "- Statement I is false and statement III is true. Indeed,  let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(-x+4)=-1+4=3 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(x+2)=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it has a removable discontinuity there). Hence, it is also not differentiable at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$",
